# Deep Research: Machine Learning Engineer — Role Definition for AI Employee

## 1. Core MLE Responsibilities & Day-to-Day Work

### 1.1 The Model Development Lifecycle

Based on extensive internal role descriptions across Atlassian (P30through P60 levels) and real operational patterns, the MLE lifecycle follows this end-to-end flow:

**Data Exploration & Problem Formulation**
- Translate business problems into technical ML solutions through experiments and analysis
- Formulate the ML problem end-to-end; identify feasibility and usability validation techniques based on PM requirements
- Research existing models in the Models Library before starting new work; fill out one-pager; present for async review
- Gather and collate validation datasets; perform periodical quantitative validation studies

**Feature Engineering**
- Develop robust feature engineering practices to ingest, process, and serve features for offline training and online inference at scale
- Work with centralized Feature Store (Tecton/MLFS) for feature definition, computation, versioning, and lineage
- Feature lifecycle: Scoping → Data collection → Feature definition → Feature value calculation → Documentation/version control → Pipeline productionization
- Ensure feature consistency between offline and online environments (a critical source of ML bugs when violated)

**Training & Experimentation**
- Design system and model architectures; conduct rigorous experimentation and model evaluations
- Train models using established frameworks (scikit-learn, XGBoost, PyTorch, TensorFlow) with MLflow experiment tracking
- Compare multiple runs by metrics (RMSE, F1, calibration), robustness across segments, and cost-performance trade-offs
- Apply qualitative checks: reasonable behavior across expected ranges, fairness/bias assessment
- Iterate through experimental → adhoc → released workflow stages with increasing compliance levels

**Model Packaging & Registration**
- Package models using ML Studio workflows and/or MSP SDK
- Register in ML Registry (Tarot v3) with proper compliance, metadata, and artifact storage
- Ensure models are serializable with supported libraries (ONNX, TensorRT conventions for Triton)
- Include environment files (conda.yaml, requirements.txt), input schema, and feature metadata

**Deployment & Serving**
- Deploy to SageMaker endpoints (real-time inference) or TEAMServe/Triton endpoints (GPU-aware, multi-model)
- Declare deployments via Micros service descriptors referencing MSP model groups and version tags
- Run post-deployment verification checks before allowing production traffic
- Coordinate with TEAMServe team for model deployment (#help-teamserve)

**Monitoring & Iteration**
- Track model performance in production via ML Lens (centralized ML observability)
- Monitor data/feature drift, data quality, model accuracy, and prediction performance
- Set up automated alerting via JSM Ops and Slack
- Retrain when performance degrades, better architectures emerge, or new data becomes available

### 1.2 The Principal/Senior MLE Distinction

From internal job descriptions:
- **P30–P40(Associate/Mid):** 70% implementation, 30% design. Work on development and implementation of ML algorithms under guidance.
- **P50(Senior):** Drive development/implementation, provide guidance to junior MLEs, broader experimentation.
- **P60 (Principal):** 70–80% engineering, 20–30% ML. Tackle largest/most complex problems, determine plans-of-attack, mentor, set code review standards, partner across engineering teams on company-wide programs.

