## 4. Common Failure Modes and Mitigation Strategies

### 4.1 Data and Model Drift

**Failure Mode:** Production data distribution shifts away from training data, causing silent model degradation.

**Detection Methods** (from internal documentation):
- Statistical tests: Kolmogorov-Smirnov test, KL divergence
- Model-based: Train classifier to distinguish training vs. new data (accuracy near 0.5= no drift)
- Embedding-based: Cosine similarity between production and training embeddings
- NLP-specific: Monitor embedding cosine similarity with threshold (e.g., 0.60)

**Mitigation:**
- ML Lens automated drift monitoring with configurable thresholds
- Metric-based retraining triggers (when drift scores breach threshold)
- Continuous training pipelines via ML Studio
- Shadow deployments to validate new models against production traffic

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 Detect drift → 🟢 Trigger approved retraining → 🟡 Deploy retrained model if metrics pass → 🔴 Escalate if retraining degrades metrics

**Source:** [Model drift identification and mitigation strategies](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CA3/pages/1953707277), [RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858)

### 4.2 Feature Pipeline Failures

**Failure Mode:** Upstream data pipeline breaks, causing missing/stale features during inference.

**Examples from internal incidents:**
- UCT minting failure (id-gatekeeper unavailable) → all scraping requests fail with400
- Feature store (Tecton) unavailable → L3 feature retrieval skipped, ranking falls back to L2
- Online/offline consistency drift → training data no longer reflects production

**Mitigation:**
- Fail-open patterns: graceful degradation when feature sources are unavailable
- Feature validation tests (schema, ranges, missing values) in pipeline
- Fallback paths for every dependency
- Observability gaps addressed with alerting on sustained error rates

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 Detect and log pipeline failure → 🟢 Activate fallback → 🟡 Investigate root cause → 🔴 Escalate if customer-facing degradation

**Source:** [3S - Operational Readiness Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SEARCH/pages/6722892863)

### 4.3 Training Instability / Job Failures

**Failure Mode:** Training jobs fail due to infrastructure issues, data problems, or hyperparameter instability.

**Examples from internal PIRs:**
- ML Studio production jobs failing when querying Socrates (data layer)
- Empty/invalid JSON causing "Input data for LocalRelation does not produce a schema" errors
- ML Studio workflow creation limits reached in production
- Databricks platform outages blocking training jobs

**Mitigation:**
- Comprehensive data validation and error handling
- Retry logic with exponential backoff
- Resource monitoring (GPU utilization, memory, disk)
- Workflow creation limit monitoring and alerting
- Clear runbooks for each failure class

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 Retry with backoff → 🟢 Skip bad data with logging → 🟡 Adjust hyperparameters → 🔴 Escalate persistent failures

**Source:** [PIR-21119](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/inckb/pages/4203708841), [Fixing the job failure issue with Data Drift Metrics](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/KYC-2941)

### 4.4 Model Serving Latency Spikes

**Failure Mode:** Inference latency exceeds SLO, degrading user experience.

**Root Causes:**
- SageMaker scale-up lag (cold starts: 3-8 minutes)
- Vegas limiter over-saturation from bursty traffic
- Resource contention from concurrent model serving
- Sidecar readiness coupling(/healthcheck dependent on OTel sidecar)

**Mitigation:**
- Circuit breaker pattern (20% failure rate threshold, 60s sliding window)
- Preemptive autoscaling for predictable traffic patterns
- Decoupling health checks from non-essential dependencies
- Per-tenant rate limiting at the edge
- Canary deployments with auto-rollback

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 Circuit breaker activates automatically → 🟢 Route to fallback → 🟡 Tune autoscaling parameters → 🔴 Customer-impacting outage requiring incident response

**Source:** [ML-Engine Circuit Breaker Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ITSOL/pages/6058351626), Tony's recent work on [OPP-202605-05]

### 4.5 Model Deployment Regression

**Failure Mode:** New model version performs worse than previous in production despite passing offline evaluation.

**Mitigation:**
- Shadow deployments (replay production traffic against new model)
- Canary rollouts with automated metric comparison
- A/B testing with statistical significance requirements
- Feature gate-based gradual rollout
- Automated rollback when online metrics degrade vs. baseline

**From ML Studio Migration approach:**
- 50/50 rollout between original and new model groups
- Metrics should not have statistically different results
- Cutover rollback: disable ML Studio workflow, delete faulty version, redeploy (~25-45 min)

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 Monitor canary metrics → 🟢 Auto-rollback on degradation → 🟡 Investigate root cause → 🔴 Escalate if rollback fails

**Source:** [ML Studio Migration Model Parity](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6087090378), [Strategies for model lifecycle management](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6590159405)

### 4.6 Experiment Reproducibility Failures

**Failure Mode:** Cannot reproduce experiment results due to missing versioning, configuration drift, or environmental differences.

**Mitigation:**
- ML Registry (Tarot v3) for model versioning with complete lineage
- Experiment tracking via MLFlow/ML Studio with full metadata
- Data version control for training datasets
- Environment pinning and containerized training
- Launchpad pipelines for reproducible pre-production validation

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 All experiments automatically versioned → 🟢 Generate reproducibility report → 🟡 Flag unreproducible results for human review

**Source:** [MLOps Tech Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~455370031/pages/2963048033), [Launchpad Pipelines](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5991558175)

### 4.7 Adversarial Inputs / Model Gaming

**Failure Mode:** Malicious or out-of-distribution inputs cause model to produce harmful/incorrect outputs.

**Examples from internal documentation:**
- Model guardrail thresholds to detect inputs far from existing clusters
- Benchmark gaming: models overfit to specific test patterns
- Pickle deserialization attacks in model supply chain

**Mitigation:**
- Input validation and anomaly detection at inference time
- Model guardrails with distance thresholds from training distribution
- Safe serialization formats (avoid pickle; use safetensors)
- Red-team testing before production deployment
- Continuous monitoring for distributional anomalies

**AI MLE Autonomy Level:** 🟢 Block anomalous inputs → 🟢 Log and alert → 🟡 Adjust guardrail thresholds → 🔴 Escalate detected adversarial patterns

**Source:** [Module3: AI/ML Vulnerabilities](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020c3e8690654d0463d99d51351ed058d5e/pages/6571655897), [Rollout model guardrails to tenants](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AIOPS-4681)

