## 5. Team Structure and Reporting Relationships

### 5.1 Organizational Models for MLE

Based on internal documentation, two primary models exist:

#### Model A: Centralized ML Platform Team

**Structure:** MLE sits in a dedicated ML Platform or Core ML team that serves the entire organization.

**Example at Atlassian:** The Core ML team in Data Technology Insights (DTI) works cross-functionally across Growth, GTM, Product, Finance, and Sales organizations.

**Advantages:**
- Shared best practices and tooling
- Efficient resource allocation across projects
- Consistent ML standards and quality bars
- Easier knowledge sharing

**Challenges:**
- "For longer term initiatives where there is more ambiguity and lack of clarity, requiring a higher level of joint ideation, exploration, communication and collaboration across multiple teams" — the centralized model struggles

**Source:** [Core ML Team Engagement2.0](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MRS/pages/2171275548)

#### Model B: Embedded in Product Teams

**Structure:** MLE embedded directly within product teams, with dotted-line to ML leadership.

**Advantages:**
- Deeper business context understanding
- Faster iteration with direct stakeholder access
- Reduced communication overhead

**Challenges:**
- Risk of duplicated effort
- Inconsistent practices across teams
- Harder to maintain platform-wide standards

#### Hybrid Model (Recommended for AI MLE Employee)

The Core ML team evolved to a hybrid approach: "Recognizing that the current engagement model works well in some circumstances but is distinctly limited in others, this page looks to address some of those challenges and propose the addition of a more embedded model so we can adapt."

**For an AI MLE Employee:** The agent should be **spawned per work item** (similar to Symphony's model) but maintain connection to the centralized ML platform for:
- Policy enforcement
- Tool access and standards compliance
- Knowledge sharing via ORBIT context layer

### 5.2 ML Platform Team Structure (Reference Architecture)

From the AI Platform Team Configuration:

```
Director of Engineering, ML Platform (Gaurav Awadhwal)
├── Engineering Manager — AI Gateway
├── Engineering Manager — MLFS / Feature Store
├── Engineering Manager — ML Studio (Training/Orchestration)
├── Engineering Manager — ML Lens / Observability & Lifecycle
└── Engineering Manager — AI Serving / Teamserve
```

**Cross-Team Roles Supporting MLEs:**
- 2Architects (specialized coordination across teams)
- 1 Product Manager (internal customer relationships, strategy)
- 0.5 Program Manager (roadmap coordination)

### 5.3 Reporting for AI MLE Employee

Based on the AI Employee Strategy:

| Relationship | Nature | Responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| **Human MLE Manager** | Primary oversight | Sets policy, reviews escalations, approves autonomy expansion |
| **ML Platform** | Platform dependency | Provides tooling, enforces standards, handles infrastructure |
| **Product Team** | Embedded collaboration | Defines business objectives, provides domain context |
| **Symphony Orchestrator** | Fleet management | Monitors health, restarts stalled agents, preserves work ownership |
| **Policy Architect** (new role) | Governance | Translates requirements into machine-executable guardrails |
| **Agent Reliability Engineer** (new role) | Operational monitoring | Monitors for logic drift and unexpected behavior |

### 5.4 On-Call Responsibilities for AI MLE

From the ML Studio On-Call proposal and DACI documentation:

**AI MLE On-Call Scope:**
- ML development issues (users can't develop/publish modules, workflow failures)
- Model serving stability (inference failures, latency degradation)
- Pipeline failures (training jobs, data ingestion, feature computation)

**Response Expectations:**
- **P2 incidents**: ACK immediately; investigate within 1 hour, any time
- **P3 incidents**: During business hours; don't ignore reliability risks
- **Disturbed pings**: Triage within 2 hours during working hours

**Key difference for AI MLE:** The AI employee handles the always-on monitoring automatically (🟢 tier), escalating to humans only when policy boundaries are hit. This eliminates the need for human overnight coverage for routine issues while maintaining human decision-making for complex incidents.

**Source:** [Regular On Call Proposal - ML Studio](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6137348671), [How To: ML Studio On-Call](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6370344384)

